<section xml:id="sec-vector-perkins">
      <title>Vector Perkins</title>
      <p>
        Today our goal is to introduce yet another notation for writing systems of linear equations. A <term>vector</term> is a list of numbers and typically denoted <m>\vec{x} = (x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{n})</m>. The set of all vectors of length <m>n</m> is denoted <m>\mathbb{R}^{n}</m>. Examples include:
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>(1,0,0,0,0,0,0,0)</mrow>
          <mrow>(0,1,0,0,0,0,0,0)</mrow>
          <mrow>(1,2,3,4,5,6)</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        A vector of length <m>n</m> is best thought of as a point in <m>n</m>-dimensional space. Given two vectors of the same length we have two core operations we can apply to them:
      </p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>Addition: <m>\vec{x} + \vec{y} = (x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{n}) + (y_{1},y_{2}, \dots, y_{n})</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Scalar Multiplication <m>\lambda\vec{x} = (\lambda x_{1}, \lambda x_{2}, \dots, \lambda x_{n})</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
      <p>
        After break we will learn that these are the two telltale signs that you can use the tools of linear algebra. Putting these two operations together, we get what is called a linear combination:
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>x_{1} \vec{v}_{1} + x_{2} \vec{v}_{2} + \dots + x_{k} \vec{v}_{k}</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        An example is <m>2(1,1) + 3(1,0) + 4(0,1) = (5,6)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Given a vector <m>\vec{b}</m>, we are often interested in rewriting as a linear combination of other vectors <m>\vec{v}_{1}, \vec{v}_{2},\dots, \vec{v}_{k}</m>. Finding coefficients to do this corresponds to an expression of the following form:
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>x_{1} \vec{v}_{1} + x_{2} \vec{v}_{2} + \dots + x_{k} \vec{v}_{k} = \vec{b}.</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        We call this a <term>linear equation with vector coefficients</term>. An example of such an equation:
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>x\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} + y\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} + z \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0\\ 1\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        If we expand this out by applying our vector operations, we get the following:
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>\begin{pmatrix} x + z \\ y \\ z\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0\\ 1 \end{pmatrix}</md>
      </p>
      <p>This is just a linear system of equations!</p>
      <theorem xml:id="thm-vector-equation-system">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Solving a <em>linear equation with vector coefficients</em> is equivalent to solving a <em>system of linear equations</em>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>
      <p>
        This also has a geometric interpretation. There are two equivalent viewpoints to think of a <m>k</m>-dimensional plane in <m>\mathbb{R}^{n}</m> that we have started to see. There is an equational form such as:
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>x+y+z = 1</md>
      </p>
      <p>or in parametric form</p>
      <p>
        <md>f(y,z) = (1-z-y,y,z).</md>
      </p>
      <p>If we write the parametric form out we get a vector equation</p>
      <p>
        <md>f(y,z) = y\begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 1\\ 0\end{pmatrix} + z \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0\\ 0\end{pmatrix}</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        We call such a function a <term>linear function with vector coefficients</term>.
      </p>
      <theorem xml:id="thm-solution-sets-images">
        <statement>
          <p>Solutions sets to linear systems correspond to images of linear functions with vector coefficients.</p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>
      <p>Let's try this for a more complicated system. Consider a linear system with augmented matrix as follows:</p>
      <p>
        <md>\begin{pmatrix}
1 &amp; 0 &amp; 3 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 \\
0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 &amp; 6
\end{pmatrix}</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        We can put in reduced row echelon by applying replacement to subtract <m>3</m> times row <m>2</m> from row <m>1</m>. This yields the matrix:
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>\begin{pmatrix}
1 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 &amp; -18 \\
0 &amp; 0 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 &amp; 6
\end{pmatrix}</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        This is in reduced row echelon form. It has two free variables <m>x_{2}</m> and <m>x_{4}</m>. Solving in terms of <m>x_{2}</m> and <m>x_{4}</m>, we have
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>x_{1} &amp;= -18 - x_{4}</mrow>
          <mrow>x_{3} &amp;= 6</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Letting <m>x = x_{2}</m> and <m>y= x_{4}</m> we get the <m>2</m>-plane in <m>\mathbb{R}^{4}</m> given by
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>f(x,y) = (-18-y, x, 6, y).</md>
      </p>
      <p>As a vector function we get</p>
      <p>
        <md>f(x,y) = \begin{pmatrix} -18 \\0\\6 \\0\end{pmatrix} + x \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\1\\0\\ 0\end{pmatrix} + y \begin{pmatrix} -1\\0\\0\\1\end{pmatrix}.</md>
      </p>
    </section>